  "body_human_readable": "### \"The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy\" Links \"42,\" Robots, Towels, and Space\n\nThe collection of terms \"sci-fi movie '42' robot poster towel space\" points to the 2005 science fiction comedy film *The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy*, based on the book series by Douglas Adams. In this story, the number \"42\" is the profound and enigmatic answer to the \"Ultimate Question of Life, the Universe, and Everything,\" as calculated by the supercomputer Deep Thought.\n\nA prominent robot character in the film is Marvin the Paranoid Android, a clinically depressed robot who is a source of much comedic relief. Posters for the movie and related merchandise frequently feature Marvin.\n\nThe significance of a towel is a running gag and a crucial element for any interstellar hitchhiker in the movie's universe. According to *The Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy*, a towel is \"about the most massively useful thing an interstellar hitchhiker can have.\" Its uses are both practical and psychological. This has led to fans celebrating \"Towel Day\" on May 25th each year in tribute to Douglas Adams.\n\nThe entire narrative unfolds across the vastness of space, following the adventures of an unwitting human, Arthur Dent, after the Earth is demolished to make way for a hyperspace bypass. He is joined by an alien friend, Ford Prefect, the two-headed galactic president Zaphod Beeblebrox, a fellow Earthling Trillian, and the ever-pessimistic Marvin. Their journey takes them to various strange and wonderful locations throughout the galaxy.",
